artifact.self_contained_problem:
Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs) are brief, intense flashes of high-energy electromagnetic radiation produced by relativistic jets launched from compact astrophysical sources. Their "prompt emission" phase exhibits a broadband spectrum, typically modeled with a dominant nonthermal component, and possibly an additional thermal (blackbody-like) component associated with the jet photosphere (the surface where the outflow becomes optically thin to its own radiation).

Identify the radiative mechanism responsible for the nonthermal component of the GRB prompt high-energy emission. Specifically, determine which of the following processes produces it:
(a) optically thin synchrotron radiation from relativistic electrons gyrating in an ordered or large-scale magnetic field;
(b) jitter radiation from relativistic electrons traversing small-scale, randomly oriented magnetic field fluctuations whose coherence length is much smaller than the electron Larmor radius;
(c) synchrotron self-Compton (SSC) emission, in which synchrotron photons are inverse-Compton scattered by the same population of relativistic electrons.

In addition, determine whether the prompt emission spectrum also contains a distinct photospheric thermal component arising from radiation released at the jet photosphere, and, if so, characterize when and how it coexists with the nonthermal component.

